and I don't think Marx is almost there,

01:21:30.930 --> 01:21:35.745
that is able to combine Silicone
Valley and central Valley.

01:21:35.745 --> 01:21:39.640
Agriculture and software.

01:21:39.640 --> 01:21:41.930
And how these two things work together.

01:21:41.930 --> 01:21:44.960
How these two things
are combined together.

01:21:44.960 --> 01:21:48.210
So these is a theoretical political effort

01:21:49.870 --> 01:21:53.090
because otherwise, I think
it's adjusted a new barstion

01:21:53.090 --> 01:21:56.100
of a theological conception of history,

01:21:56.100 --> 01:22:01.100
and in the 20th century,
industrial labor was hegemonic,

01:22:01.207 --> 01:22:04.170
and agriculture was a
backward or residual.

01:22:04.170 --> 01:22:07.030
Now the material labor is hegemonic

01:22:07.030 --> 01:22:08.930
and the industrial labor is a residual.

01:22:08.930 --> 01:22:09.803
I don't buy it.

01:22:11.330 --> 01:22:15.170
These different forms of
exploitations co-exist.

01:22:15.170 --> 01:22:18.093
What do we have to explain
is the co existence,

01:22:19.490 --> 01:22:22.820
not the fact that the one is hegemonic

01:22:22.820 --> 01:22:26.803
or will be hegemonic, or is a
tender tendensually hegemonic.

01:22:28.190 --> 01:22:32.283
This is my, I won't read
Marx and without teleology.

01:22:33.200 --> 01:22:38.200
And I think in a lot of my Italian fellows

01:22:38.680 --> 01:22:43.680
there is a, still some teleology,
when they think in terms

01:22:44.530 --> 01:22:48.700
of immaterial labor and what
is the tendency, and so on.

01:22:49.717 --> 01:22:51.320
- This is a time to mention

01:22:51.320 --> 01:22:54.440
again, the book on Marx's Temporalities,

01:22:54.440 --> 01:22:58.083
which is is a crusade against
the teleological reading.

01:22:59.580 --> 01:23:02.707
Okay, there's a couple of other questions.

01:23:04.970 --> 01:23:06.760
One is a comment. But
why don't we do these two

01:23:06.760 --> 01:23:08.370
and then we'll call it a night.

01:23:08.370 --> 01:23:10.200
This is Phillip Walshshutter.

01:23:10.200 --> 01:23:15.200
Phillip do you want to
present your comment question?

01:23:21.870 --> 01:23:23.050
- Can you hear me?

01:23:23.050 --> 01:23:24.080
- [Paul North] Yes.

01:23:24.080 --> 01:23:26.663
- Okay, hi, Marx, greetings from Red Bay.

01:23:29.731 --> 01:23:34.040
I'm very taken with
your focus on the body,

01:23:34.040 --> 01:23:37.140
and this sense of how capitalism tie,

01:23:37.140 --> 01:23:40.090
you become an appendage of the machine.

01:23:40.090 --> 01:23:41.973
It formats your time.

01:23:43.030 --> 01:23:45.760
It's a focus that I see
in your work and postone

01:23:45.760 --> 01:23:47.720
and a lot of different people.

01:23:47.720 --> 01:23:52.720
And it's a focus away from a
mode of attacking capitalism

01:23:53.060 --> 01:23:57.730
which starts to establish
exploitation by trying to trot

01:23:57.730 --> 01:24:00.660
out quantities and doing
mathematical equations,

01:24:00.660 --> 01:24:04.020
like so many hours are stolen each day.

01:24:04.020 --> 01:24:07.170
And that seems to be right on the terrain

01:24:07.170 --> 01:24:11.100
that neoclassical economists
love, because it can end

01:24:11.100 --> 01:24:14.060
in some endless dispute,
but when we're focusing

01:24:14.060 --> 01:24:16.500
on what happens to the body and the fact

01:24:16.500 --> 01:24:19.590
that it's limitless and it never stops,

01:24:19.590 --> 01:24:21.610
it's going on and on which means,

01:24:21.610 --> 01:24:23.680
you're going to work all your life,

01:24:23.680 --> 01:24:27.580
and if there's a COVID
plague and you're a home

01:24:27.580 --> 01:24:30.630
and you risk dying, they're
gonna push you to get out,

01:24:30.630 --> 01:24:31.870
so you could work.

01:24:31.870 --> 01:24:35.140
It seems to me that that
particular focus in Marx,

01:24:35.140 --> 01:24:40.140
and it's all there in this
notion of capital as this kind

01:24:40.490 --> 01:24:45.490
of automatic MCM prime generator,
that just keeps on going,

01:24:45.610 --> 01:24:48.340
and yeah, it doesn't really
care about your body.

01:24:48.340 --> 01:24:51.410
It's the skin that has
labor power around it,

01:24:51.410 --> 01:24:54.070
and if it could extract
that it would do it

01:24:54.070 --> 01:24:55.980
but it's quite happy to work you to death

01:24:55.980 --> 01:24:57.030
and have someone else.

01:24:57.030 --> 01:24:59.510
So it seems to me that your entire focus

01:24:59.510 --> 01:25:02.780
is a much more effective organizing angle

01:25:02.780 --> 01:25:05.700
just in terms of trying
to make people realize

01:25:05.700 --> 01:25:10.700
what capitalism is, and
what can't change about it.

01:25:10.770 --> 01:25:13.639
Which is precisely the limitlessness.

01:25:13.639 --> 01:25:16.943
That's kind of found still
in drive to keep going.

01:25:17.840 --> 01:25:20.470
So it's more of a comment than a question

01:25:20.470 --> 01:25:23.320
and then encouragement saying as always,

01:25:23.320 --> 01:25:24.875
I wait your next book Marx.

01:25:24.875 --> 01:25:26.910
(laughs)

01:25:26.910 --> 01:25:28.160
- Well, thank you Philip.

01:25:31.293 --> 01:25:32.290
Yeah.

01:25:32.290 --> 01:25:37.290
You know especially after I
published Marx Temporalities,

01:25:40.690 --> 01:25:44.760
I had the opportunity to go
back to Marx again and again,

01:25:44.760 --> 01:25:47.930
but it wasn't more about the,

01:25:47.930 --> 01:25:49.770
what is the political stake in this book?

01:25:49.770 --> 01:25:54.620
How we can probably read
this book today politically.

01:25:54.620 --> 01:25:59.280
And as I said and I think
it was pretty clear,

01:25:59.280 --> 01:26:01.560
at the end of my presentation,

01:26:01.560 --> 01:26:05.280
I think that we have a
problem, and the problem one

01:26:05.280 --> 01:26:06.470
we have many problems,

01:26:06.470 --> 01:26:10.077
political problems and economic problems.

01:26:10.077 --> 01:26:12.010
But one of them is that,

01:26:12.010 --> 01:26:15.840
we think politics in terms of empty.

01:26:15.840 --> 01:26:18.083
Once I asked my students,

01:26:19.147 --> 01:26:23.297
"okay, so you don't like the
capitalist model production.

01:26:23.297 --> 01:26:24.217
"Now what?"

01:26:26.490 --> 01:26:28.423
We want to, abolish the value.

01:26:29.610 --> 01:26:31.070
What does it mean?

01:26:31.070 --> 01:26:33.240
I'm serious, what does it mean?

01:26:33.240 --> 01:26:37.053
Oh, we want to stop the
valorization, but what does it mean?

01:26:38.780 --> 01:26:42.213
I really don't know exactly what it means.

01:26:44.940 --> 01:26:46.843
We don't want to produce commodities.

01:26:47.740 --> 01:26:49.253
Yeah, why, how?

01:26:51.250 --> 01:26:53.570
It's kind of, so we have a list

01:26:53.570 --> 01:26:58.570
of things which denote the
capitalist mode of production.

01:26:58.580 --> 01:27:02.700
And we think that by
attacking all these pieces

01:27:02.700 --> 01:27:05.743
in the list of things,
we have something else.

01:27:07.832 --> 01:27:09.150
I'm not sure about that.

01:27:09.150 --> 01:27:13.897
For that reason, my conclusion
was, "Look I think that Marx,

01:27:13.897 --> 01:27:15.527
"could have write this book,

01:27:15.527 --> 01:27:19.037
"because in the 19 century,

01:27:19.037 --> 01:27:24.037
"he could see an attendative associations.

01:27:24.137 --> 01:27:26.297
"How workers organize themselves.

01:27:26.297 --> 01:27:31.297
"How they organize their whole
life in a different way."

01:27:31.370 --> 01:27:34.600
So that was the positive
thing is not only empty.

01:27:34.600 --> 01:27:38.690
They had a kind of a pre
figuration anticipation.

01:27:38.690 --> 01:27:41.230
You can call it whatever you want,

01:27:41.230 --> 01:27:43.873
but they had an alternative in action.

01:27:44.720 --> 01:27:47.543
And that was a theory in practice.

01:27:48.410 --> 01:27:51.110
Marx has only to develop the theory

01:27:51.110 --> 01:27:54.730
that was already embedded
in their practices.

01:27:54.730 --> 01:27:57.873
I think today we should
do something similar.

01:27:58.850 --> 01:28:03.720
Where is now the anticipation
of a different form of life.

01:28:03.720 --> 01:28:06.840
And we have to extract theory from there,

01:28:06.840 --> 01:28:11.840
instead of developing a
new theory of the empty,

01:28:12.390 --> 01:28:13.930
capitalist mode of production.

01:28:13.930 --> 01:28:18.150
Or we the entire humanity
should be against

01:28:18.150 --> 01:28:19.670
the capitalist mode of production,

01:28:19.670 --> 01:28:21.563
because we are all exploited.

01:28:25.370 --> 01:28:26.203
Does it work?

01:28:29.990 --> 01:28:34.780
Without associations, without
real forms of workers,

01:28:35.730 --> 01:28:36.980
does it work?

01:28:36.980 --> 01:28:40.327
Then people started dreaming,
"Oh, but we need a party."

01:28:41.680 --> 01:28:43.550
Okay, sorry I become polemical.

01:28:43.550 --> 01:28:47.460
I don't want to be because
I agree with the feeling,

01:28:47.460 --> 01:28:50.610
otherwise, people think I
am disagreement with them.

01:28:50.610 --> 01:28:53.080
I agree with him, but I
think we have open questions.

01:28:53.080 --> 01:28:56.450
I think today we have a
much more open questions

01:28:56.450 --> 01:28:57.785
than answers.

01:28:57.785 --> 01:29:01.210
And I think when we read this
book, Capital volume one,

01:29:01.210 --> 01:29:04.483
we should define the
questions more than answers.

01:29:05.920 --> 01:29:10.660
- I want to give a chance
for Alessandro Fornazzari,

01:29:10.660 --> 01:29:12.563
to ask a final question.

01:29:13.680 --> 01:29:15.833
Can we unmute Alessandro?

01:29:17.840 --> 01:29:19.183
- [Alessandro] Yeah, hello.

01:29:20.730 --> 01:29:25.450
So I had a question regarding
the move from circulation

01:29:25.450 --> 01:29:26.910
to production, that in the chapters

01:29:26.910 --> 01:29:30.180
that you were working through.

01:29:30.180 --> 01:29:32.850
We get this move from leaving
the sphere of circulation

01:29:32.850 --> 01:29:35.203
into the hidden abode.

01:29:36.280 --> 01:29:39.063
The promise that the secret
is somehow isn't a factory.

01:29:39.950 --> 01:29:42.250
And I guess I'm wondering,

01:29:42.250 --> 01:29:45.040
up to what point do we
need to relativize that?

01:29:45.040 --> 01:29:48.390
And I'm thinking is circulation
necessarily the sphere

01:29:48.390 --> 01:29:49.810
of mystification?

01:29:49.810 --> 01:29:51.690
And then if that's the case, what do we do

01:29:51.690 --> 01:29:54.760
with volume two, which
is sort of an intensified

01:29:54.760 --> 01:29:56.923
return to the sphere of circulation?

01:29:59.910 --> 01:30:01.413
- Yeah good question.

01:30:12.920 --> 01:30:15.753
There is an elegant way to answer.

01:30:19.796 --> 01:30:21.053
And I borrow,

01:30:23.220 --> 01:30:28.220
maybe something from Tomba's

01:30:28.760 --> 01:30:33.313
who brought the book on
time, in Marx Capital.

01:30:34.240 --> 01:30:39.240
One can say that there are
three layers not stages.

01:30:39.410 --> 01:30:42.220
Three layers of analysis.

01:30:42.220 --> 01:30:47.220
In capital volume one Marx
is trying to understand time,

01:30:47.530 --> 01:30:49.173
at the level of a production.

01:30:50.190 --> 01:30:53.400
How time is crucial in
the valorization process,

01:30:53.400 --> 01:30:55.420
but within production.

01:30:55.420 --> 01:30:57.070
In the volume two,

01:30:57.070 --> 01:31:01.770
is understanding how time is dominating.

01:31:05.997 --> 01:31:10.997
Is a crucial in the circulation
and competition of capitals.

01:31:11.240 --> 01:31:15.240
And then we have a Capital volume three,

01:31:15.240 --> 01:31:20.240
in which Marx combines these two levels.

01:31:20.380 --> 01:31:22.830
And basically he develops,

01:31:22.830 --> 01:31:26.860
he really develops an idea
of a competition of capitals.

01:31:26.860 --> 01:31:31.100
And I think this is very
innovative even for Marx himself,

01:31:33.025 --> 01:31:37.317
because when he brought the Green resa

01:31:38.500 --> 01:31:43.500
which are I go back to polemical point,

01:31:43.990 --> 01:31:48.990
which are basically the text
that the people who are talking

01:31:49.670 --> 01:31:52.830
about immaterial labor, law.

01:31:52.830 --> 01:31:56.970
In the Green resa you don't
find the competition of capital.

01:31:56.970 --> 01:32:01.970
Marx is working, Mika and Eilish
that I think he would come

01:32:02.220 --> 01:32:03.163
to give a paper.

01:32:04.162 --> 01:32:06.060
He wrote an article about that.

01:32:06.060 --> 01:32:08.210
In the Green resa, Marx is talking

01:32:08.210 --> 01:32:11.270
about capital in general,
he's very abstract.

01:32:11.270 --> 01:32:13.333
It's like there is only one capital.

01:32:14.580 --> 01:32:19.540
Then in the 61, 63, he
developed his ideas,

01:32:19.540 --> 01:32:22.330
and he understood that the
actually there is not such

01:32:22.330 --> 01:32:24.020
a thing as a capital in generals

01:32:24.020 --> 01:32:26.730
but there in general, but
there are many capitals.

01:32:26.730 --> 01:32:30.690
And then he has to address the real issue

01:32:30.690 --> 01:32:33.113
of the competition among these capitals.

01:32:33.980 --> 01:32:37.810
And this is what makes
things extremely concrete.

01:32:37.810 --> 01:32:42.810
So my answer is we have
to read these three books

01:32:43.530 --> 01:32:48.420
as a three level, three
layers of analysis.

01:32:48.420 --> 01:32:50.663
In which in a certain way you find,

01:32:51.944 --> 01:32:56.944
the concrete analysis of many
capitals in the world market,

01:33:01.290 --> 01:33:02.413
in the third volume.

01:33:03.820 --> 01:33:04.757
But it's just that,

01:33:04.757 --> 01:33:09.757
its a kind of a way to
develop his analysis.

01:33:11.250 --> 01:33:13.690
But then, and then it's
possible to go further.

01:33:13.690 --> 01:33:15.433
But then we're going philology,
because it's also true

01:33:15.433 --> 01:33:20.433
that the Marx brought Capital
volume three before volume one

01:33:20.500 --> 01:33:22.870
and there are many different issues.

01:33:22.870 --> 01:33:27.843
And then one can ask him, why
did he stop writing capital?

01:33:30.314 --> 01:33:32.770
And some people say he
stopped writing capital

01:33:32.770 --> 01:33:34.900
after the parties commune.

01:33:34.900 --> 01:33:39.900
And then he was more and more
interested in none capitalist

01:33:40.450 --> 01:33:42.670
for most forms of society.

01:33:42.670 --> 01:33:47.670
He start reading a lot
of India, and the commune

01:33:49.090 --> 01:33:52.280
and the common possession,
and in the villages.

01:33:52.280 --> 01:33:54.510
On Russia and the Optina,

01:33:54.510 --> 01:33:59.130
and me, he started reading
history and the non-capitalist

01:33:59.130 --> 01:34:00.990
forms of production.

01:34:00.990 --> 01:34:01.870
Why short time?

01:34:01.870 --> 01:34:05.170
So because I think he
had the to understand

01:34:05.170 --> 01:34:08.203
what an old capitalistic
society should look like.

01:34:09.090 --> 01:34:12.060
And second question,

01:34:12.060 --> 01:34:17.060
how is possible to skip
capitalists in society

01:34:19.560 --> 01:34:24.053
which are not organized in a
capitalist mode of production.

01:34:25.140 --> 01:34:26.550
And that was a crucial question

01:34:26.550 --> 01:34:28.670
because there were people who claim

01:34:28.670 --> 01:34:33.270
to be Marxist who thought
already in the 19th century

01:34:33.270 --> 01:34:38.270
that it was necessary to go
through every single stage.

01:34:38.570 --> 01:34:40.280
And in order to achieve social reason

01:34:40.280 --> 01:34:44.180
first you have to be happy to is society.

01:34:44.180 --> 01:34:47.913
And Marx was in doubt.

01:34:48.845 --> 01:34:51.970
And in that a beautiful
letter to the authority

01:34:51.970 --> 01:34:53.940
he said, actually, it's not true.

01:34:53.940 --> 01:34:57.070
You can use the Russian option just

01:34:57.070 --> 01:35:00.930
in order to skip the
capitalist mode of production.

01:35:00.930 --> 01:35:03.900
So why am I saying that?

01:35:03.900 --> 01:35:07.050
I'm saying that just in
order to emphasize one point

01:35:07.050 --> 01:35:09.013
the project is an open project.

01:35:10.895 --> 01:35:14.287
There is no way we can read
this book dogmatically.

01:35:15.450 --> 01:35:16.667
It's an open project.

01:35:16.667 --> 01:35:21.120
And I think today we
should find questions,

01:35:21.120 --> 01:35:24.393
more than answers in this book.

01:35:25.250 --> 01:35:28.710
And yeah, this is what I think.

01:35:28.710 --> 01:35:29.750
- Thank you so much.

01:35:29.750 --> 01:35:33.090
That's really, it's the first,
I think hopeful presentation

01:35:33.090 --> 01:35:35.390
on Marx that I've ever heard.

01:35:35.390 --> 01:35:39.250
And I can see a whole other texts there.

01:35:39.250 --> 01:35:42.700
So I really appreciate
that opening up a set

01:35:42.700 --> 01:35:47.110
of positions from which
to critique capitalists,

01:35:47.110 --> 01:35:48.860
that don't turn capitalism,

01:35:48.860 --> 01:35:51.550
that don't turn it into a big monolith,

01:35:51.550 --> 01:35:53.410
you'd have to destroy all at once.

01:35:53.410 --> 01:35:56.910
Finding positions within it
that are already outside of it.

01:35:56.910 --> 01:35:58.140
I think that's really helpful.

01:35:58.140 --> 01:36:01.550
So I wanna thank you on
behalf of everyone who's here.

01:36:01.550 --> 01:36:03.400
This is another time where
there would be plenty

01:36:03.400 --> 01:36:04.233
of applause.

01:36:04.233 --> 01:36:09.200
You'll see them hands clapping,
and thanks again for coming.

01:36:10.403 --> 01:36:11.793
- Thank you very much.

01:36:11.793 --> 01:36:14.023
And thank you for your
comments, your questions.

